PART 3: HOW BABEL WORKS

================================================================================

The Babel Compilation Pipeline

A simplified Babel pipeline has three important phases:

PHASE 1
PARSE
  ↓
AST
  ↓
PHASE 2
TRANSFORM
  ↓
TRANSFORMED AST
  ↓
PHASE 3
GENERATE
  ↓
JAVASCRIPT OUTPUT


Quick table:

Phase       | Purpose
------------|----------------------------------------------
Parse       | Convert source code into an AST
Transform   | Change the AST using transformations/plugins
Generate    | Convert transformed AST back into source code


================================================================================

PHASE 1: PARSE

================================================================================

Babel parses source code into an AST.

What is an AST?

AST = Abstract Syntax Tree

An AST is a structured representation of source code.

AST Characteristic    | Meaning
----------------------|--------------------------------------------
Tree Structure        | Represents relationships in source syntax
Compiler Friendly     | Allows tools to inspect code programmatically
Transformable         | Nodes can be modified
Source Representation | Represents the meaning/structure of code


Example JSX:

<h1>Hello</h1>


Highly simplified conceptual AST:

{
  type: 'JSXElement',

  openingElement: {
    name: 'h1',
    attributes: []
  },

  children: [
    {
      type: 'JSXText',
      value: 'Hello'
    }
  ]
}


Easy flow:

SOURCE CODE
<h1>Hello</h1>

       ↓ PARSE

AST
JSXElement
├── h1
└── Hello


================================================================================

PHASE 2: TRANSFORM

================================================================================

Babel transformations operate on the AST.

What Are Babel Plugins?

Babel plugins can perform specific code transformations.

Plugin Characteristic  | Description
-----------------------|--------------------------------------------
Focused                | Handles specific syntax/transformation
AST-Based              | Works with parsed code representation
Composable             | Multiple plugins can work together
Configurable           | Enabled according to project requirements


Example plugin:

@babel/plugin-transform-react-jsx

Simplified JSX Transformation

Before:

JSXElement

<h1>Hello</h1>


Conceptually after a classic JSX transform:

CallExpression

React.createElement(
  'h1',
  null,
  'Hello'
)


Simplified representation:

{
  type: 'CallExpression',
  callee: {
    name: 'React.createElement'
  },
  arguments: [
    'h1',
    null,
    'Hello'
  ]
}


================================================================================

PHASE 3: GENERATE

================================================================================

After transformation, code is generated from the transformed structure.

Conceptually:

TRANSFORMED AST
      ↓
CODE GENERATOR
      ↓
JAVASCRIPT


Classic output example:

React.createElement(
  'h1',
  null,
  'Hello'
);


================================================================================

COMPLETE BABEL PIPELINE EXAMPLE

================================================================================

Input:

<h1>Hello World</h1>


Flow:

JSX SOURCE
<h1>Hello World</h1>

       ↓

1. PARSE

       ↓

AST
JSXElement
├── h1
└── Hello World

       ↓

2. TRANSFORM

       ↓

CallExpression
React.createElement(...)

       ↓

3. GENERATE

       ↓

JAVASCRIPT
React.createElement(
  'h1',
  null,
  'Hello World'
)


Memory trick:

PARSE → TRANSFORM → GENERATE


