PART 4: JSX TO JAVASCRIPT TRANSFORMATION EXAMPLES

================================================================================

The following examples use the classic JSX transform to make the relationship between JSX and React elements easy to understand.

================================================================================

Example 1: Simple Element

================================================================================

JSX Input
const element =
  <h1>Hello World</h1>;

Classic Transformation
const element =
  React.createElement(
    'h1',
    null,
    'Hello World'
  );

Explanation
JSX Part          | createElement Argument
------------------|--------------------------------
<h1>              | 'h1' → element type
No attributes     | null → no props
Hello World       | 'Hello World' → child


Syntax:

React.createElement(
  type,
  props,
  ...children
)


================================================================================

Example 2: Element with Attributes

================================================================================

JSX
const button = (
  <button
    className="btn"
    onClick={handleClick}
  >
    Click Me
  </button>
);

Classic Transformation
const button =
  React.createElement(
    'button',
    {
      className: 'btn',
      onClick: handleClick
    },
    'Click Me'
  );

Explanation
JSX                       | Transformation
--------------------------|------------------------------------
button                    | 'button'
className="btn"           | className: 'btn'
onClick={handleClick}     | onClick: handleClick
Click Me                  | Child content


================================================================================

Example 3: Nested Elements

================================================================================

JSX
const card = (
  <div>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Description</p>
  </div>
);

Classic Transformation
const card =
  React.createElement(
    'div',
    null,

    React.createElement(
      'h1',
      null,
      'Title'
    ),

    React.createElement(
      'p',
      null,
      'Description'
    )
  );

Structure
div
├── h1
│   └── Title
│
└── p
    └── Description


Nested JSX becomes nested React element creation.

================================================================================

Example 4: Variables and Expressions

================================================================================

JSX
const name = 'Alice';

const greeting =
  <h1>Hello {name}!</h1>;


Conceptually, the JavaScript expression name is preserved as part of the generated element's child data.

Classic-style simplified output:

const name = 'Alice';

const greeting =
  React.createElement(
    'h1',
    null,
    'Hello ',
    name,
    '!'
  );


Main idea:

{name}
   ↓
JavaScript Expression
   ↓
Current Value
   ↓
Displayed in UI


================================================================================

Example 5: Conditional Rendering

================================================================================

JSX
const message = (
  <div>

    {isLoggedIn
      ? <h1>Welcome!</h1>
      : <h1>Please login</h1>
    }

  </div>
);

Classic Transformation
const message =
  React.createElement(
    'div',
    null,

    isLoggedIn
      ? React.createElement(
          'h1',
          null,
          'Welcome!'
        )
      : React.createElement(
          'h1',
          null,
          'Please login'
        )
  );

What Changes?
Part                     | Result
-------------------------|----------------------------------
Ternary operator         | Remains JavaScript logic
<h1>Welcome!</h1>        | Becomes React element
<h1>Please login</h1>    | Becomes React element


================================================================================

Example 6: List Rendering

================================================================================

JSX
const items = [
  'Apple',
  'Banana',
  'Orange'
];

const list = (
  <ul>

    {items.map(item => (
      <li key={item}>
        {item}
      </li>
    ))}

  </ul>
);

Classic Transformation
const items = [
  'Apple',
  'Banana',
  'Orange'
];

const list =
  React.createElement(
    'ul',
    null,

    items.map(item =>
      React.createElement(
        'li',
        { key: item },
        item
      )
    )
  );

Explanation
JSX / JavaScript       | Transformation
-----------------------|--------------------------------------
<ul>                   | React element
map()                  | Remains JavaScript array logic
<li>                   | React element
key={item}             | Element key configuration
{item}                 | JavaScript value


================================================================================

Example 7: Inline Styles

================================================================================

JSX
const styled = (
  <div
    style={{
      color: 'red',
      fontSize: '16px'
    }}
  >
    Styled Text
  </div>
);

Classic Transformation
const styled =
  React.createElement(
    'div',
    {
      style: {
        color: 'red',
        fontSize: '16px'
      }
    },
    'Styled Text'
  );

Explanation
JSX                     | JavaScript Representation
------------------------|------------------------------------
style={...}             | style property
color: 'red'            | Object property
fontSize: '16px'        | Object property
Styled Text             | Child


The style value remains a JavaScript object.

================================================================================

Example 8: Fragment

================================================================================

JSX
const fragment = (
  <>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Content</p>
  </>
);


Classic conceptual transformation:

const fragment =
  React.createElement(
    React.Fragment,
    null,

    React.createElement(
      'h1',
      null,
      'Title'
    ),

    React.createElement(
      'p',
      null,
      'Content'
    )
  );

Explanation
JSX            | Classic Representation
---------------|----------------------------------
<>...</>       | React.Fragment
<h1>           | Child
<p>            | Child
DOM Wrapper    | No extra wrapper DOM element


