PART 8: PRACTICAL EXAMPLES - BEFORE AND AFTER

================================================================================

The following compiled examples continue using the classic JSX runtime because it makes the relationship with React.createElement() easy to study.

================================================================================

REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE 1: COUNTER COMPONENT

================================================================================

JSX - What You Write
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  return (
    <div className="counter">

      <h1>
        Count: {count}
      </h1>

      <button
        onClick={() =>
          setCount(count + 1)
        }
      >
        Increment
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

Classic Compiled Form
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  return React.createElement(
    'div',

    {
      className: 'counter'
    },

    React.createElement(
      'h1',
      null,
      'Count: ',
      count
    ),

    React.createElement(
      'button',

      {
        onClick: () =>
          setCount(count + 1)
      },

      'Increment'
    )
  );
}

What Changed?
Original JSX             | Classic Transformation
-------------------------|------------------------------------------
<div>                    | createElement('div', ...)
<h1>                     | createElement('h1', ...)
<button>                 | createElement('button', ...)
className                | Property preserved
onClick                  | Handler preserved
{count}                  | JavaScript value preserved
Nested JSX               | Nested element calls


