PART 9: COMMON BABEL TRANSFORMATIONS

================================================================================

Babel can also transform newer JavaScript syntax depending on the project's configuration and target browsers/environments.

================================================================================

Transformation 1: Arrow Functions

================================================================================

Modern JavaScript:

const add = (a, b) => a + b;


Possible older-style transformation:

var add = function(a, b) {
  return a + b;
};

Modern Syntax      | Older-Style Equivalent
-------------------|--------------------------------
(a, b) => a + b    | function(a, b) { return a + b }


Whether this transformation occurs depends on configured targets/plugins.

================================================================================

Transformation 2: const / let

================================================================================

Input:

const name = 'Alice';
let age = 25;


With sufficiently old targets, tooling may transform declarations into older-compatible syntax such as:

var name = 'Alice';
var age = 25;


Modern target environments may not require this transformation.

================================================================================

Transformation 3: Template Literals

================================================================================

Input:

const name = 'Alice';

const greeting =
  `Hello ${name}!`;


Possible transformation:

var name = 'Alice';

var greeting =
  'Hello ' + name + '!';


================================================================================

Transformation 4: Spread Syntax

================================================================================

Input:

const arr1 = [
  1,
  2,
  3
];

const arr2 = [
  ...arr1,
  4,
  5
];


An older-target transformation can use helper logic or operations with equivalent behavior.

A simplified conceptual equivalent is:

var arr1 = [1, 2, 3];

var arr2 =
  arr1.concat([
    4,
    5
  ]);


The exact emitted Babel output depends on configuration, Babel version, plugins, and target environment.

